WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.280
How do you read and

2
00:00:01.280 --> 00:00:03.260
trace a Turing machine? Its

3
00:00:03.260 --> 00:00:05.160
finite control reads and writes

4
00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:06.860
an unbounded tape while a

5
00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:09.040
head moves between cells. We

6
00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:10.900
will translate a configuration into

7
00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:12.580
a picture and perform read,

8
00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:14.940
write, state change and head

9
00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:16.660
movement as separate operations.

10
00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:18.960
The formal machine has state

11
00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:21.740
set, input alphabet, tape alphabet,

12
00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:24.780
transition function, start state, accept

13
00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:27.380
state and reject state. The

14
00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:29.140
basic course transition writes a

15
00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:30.700
symbol and moves left or

16
00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:32.300
right. The blank is a

17
00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:33.420
tape symbol that can be

18
00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:35.020
written, but it is not

19
00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:36.480
an input-alphabet symbol.

20
00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:38.300
In configuration notation u q

21
00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:41.160
v, the head scans the

22
00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:43.020
first symbol of v. The

23
00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:44.880
whole tape segment represented is

24
00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:46.680
u followed by v, and

25
00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:47.960
q is a state marker

26
00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:49.420
rather than a tape character.

27
00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:51.040
Include a blank when needed

28
00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:52.280
to show the scanned cell

29
00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:54.380
beyond the rightmost nonblank symbol.

30
00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:56.620
Use the lecture transition from

31
00:00:56.620 --> 00:00:58.540
q two reading b: enter

32
00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:00.640
q three, write x, and

33
00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:02.700
move left. Starting with a

34
00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:04.500
a, state q two, then

35
00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:06.380
b a, the head initially

36
00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:08.580
scans the b. First replace

37
00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:10.220
that b with x; it

38
00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:11.260
is not the a to

39
00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:12.740
the left that gets overwritten.

40
00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:15.120
After moving left, the head

41
00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:16.480
scans the second a and

42
00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:17.500
the state is q three.

43
00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:19.760
The resulting notation is a,

44
00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:21.640
then q three, then a

45
00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:23.140
x a. This matches the

46
00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:24.800
unchanged first a, the newly

47
00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:26.100
scanned second a and the

48
00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:27.700
overwritten x to its right.

49
00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:29.940
For a right move, write

50
00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:31.260
the replacement in the current

51
00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:32.900
cell before advancing the head.

52
00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:34.480
If the head reaches a

53
00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:37.060
previously unshown cell, that cell

54
00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:39.020
is blank. Never move the

55
00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:40.740
state marker first and then

56
00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:42.840
overwrite whichever symbol happens to

57
00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:44.080
appear after it in your

58
00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:45.140
new written notation.

59
00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:47.400
At the left end of

60
00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:49.900
the course’s semi-infinite tape, a

61
00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:51.620
requested left move leaves the

62
00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:53.540
head at that boundary. Therefore

63
00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:55.580
consecutive configurations can have the

64
00:01:55.580 --> 00:01:57.580
same head location even though

65
00:01:57.580 --> 00:01:59.500
the ordinary transition directions are

66
00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:01.200
only left and right. A

67
00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:03.100
fully general stay operation is

68
00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:05.200
a different model convention, though

69
00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:06.240
it can be simulated.

70
00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:08.460
Entering an accept or reject

71
00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:10.860
state halts the computation immediately.

72
00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:12.940
A machine that never reaches

73
00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:14.620
either has a nonhalting run,

74
00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:16.040
even if its tape and

75
00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:17.120
head do not repeat a

76
00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:20.220
previous complete configuration. An infinite

77
00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:22.140
tape permits looping behaviour that

78
00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:23.880
continually visits new cells.

79
00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:26.680
A recognizer must accept every

80
00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:28.260
member and never accept a

81
00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:30.340
nonmember, but may loop on

82
00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:32.520
nonmembers. A decider must halt

83
00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:34.420
on every input, accepting or

84
00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:37.320
rejecting correctly. Describing a machine

85
00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:39.260
that works only on successful

86
00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:41.900
examples is therefore insufficient to

87
00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:42.880
prove decidability.

88
00:02:43.820 --> 00:02:45.140
Things to take note of.

89
00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:47.180
Keep state symbols separate from

90
00:02:47.180 --> 00:02:49.300
tape symbols, overwrite the old

91
00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:50.940
head cell, and apply the

92
00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:53.640
stated boundary convention. Distinguish input

93
00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:55.360
from tape alphabets and recognition

94
00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:57.180
from decision, and stop tracing

95
00:02:57.180 --> 00:02:58.180
as soon as a halting

96
00:02:58.180 --> 00:02:58.920
state is entered.
